// ═══ CORRECT: log.error with throwable as LAST argument ═══
try {
    tradeService.settle("TRD-001");
} catch (TradeSettlementException e) {
    log.error("Settlement failed for trade {}", "TRD-001", e);
    // SLF4J detects: last arg is Throwable → prints FULL stack trace!
    // Output:
    // ERROR Settlement failed for trade TRD-001
    // com.bank.TradeSettlementException: Settlement failed for TRD-001
    //   at TradeService.settle(TradeService.java:42)
    //   at TradeController.execute(TradeController.java:18)
    // Caused by: java.sql.SQLException: Connection refused
    //   at HikariPool.getConnection(HikariPool.java:128)
    // Caused by: java.net.ConnectException: Connection refused
    //   at Socket.connect(Socket.java:591)
}

// (Avoid) WRONG 1: getMessage() — loses stack trace and cause chain!
log.error("Error: " + exception.getMessage());
// Output: "Error: Settlement failed for TRD-001"
// NO stack trace! NO line numbers! NO "Caused by"! USELESS for debugging!

// (Avoid) WRONG 2: toString() — slightly better but still no stack trace
log.error("Error: {}", exception.toString());
// Output: "Error: TradeSettlementException: Settlement failed for TRD-001"
// Still NO stack trace! NO line numbers!

// (Avoid) WRONG 3: printStackTrace() — not production-safe!
exception.printStackTrace();
// Writes to System.err (not log file!). No timestamp. Not thread-safe.
// Output goes to console, not ELK/Splunk. LOST in production!

// ═══ READING the "Caused by:" chain ═══
// TradeSettlementException: Settlement failed for TRD-001     ← BUSINESS context
//   at TradeService.settle(TradeService.java:42)               ← WHERE in your code
//   at TradeController.execute(TradeController.java:18)
// Caused by: java.sql.SQLException: Connection refused          ← TECHNICAL layer
//   at HikariPool.getConnection(HikariPool.java:128)
// Caused by: java.net.ConnectException: Connection refused      ← ROOT CAUSE!
//   at java.net.Socket.connect(Socket.java:591)

// ROOT CAUSE = LAST "Caused by:" = ConnectException: Connection refused
// This tells you: the database server is down! Fix the DB, not the code.
```

